1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,410
Welcome to Class 5: Slurm acceptance patterns.

2
00:00:03,410 --> 00:00:07,794
This video introduces the core decisions and working patterns.

3
00:00:07,794 --> 00:00:14,614
Watch the complete lesson first, then use the written class page for copyable commands,

4
00:00:14,614 --> 00:00:17,050
exercises, and detailed reference material.

5
00:00:17,050 --> 00:00:23,993
GPU work follows the same principle: request the current GPU partition in a batch job and

6
00:00:23,993 --> 00:00:27,031
keep interactive GPU exploration attended and bounded.

7
00:00:27,031 --> 00:00:32,553
The following screenshot was captured from the RCC scheduler on 25 July 2026.

8
00:00:32,553 --> 00:00:38,500
It shows what sinfo looks like, but the available nodes and partitions can change.

9
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:41,474
Run sinfo yourself before choosing a partition.

10
00:00:41,474 --> 00:00:46,572
A minimal batch script is: Slurm normally allocates the requested CPU, memory,

11
00:00:46,572 --> 00:00:49,546
GPU, and time on a suitable node.

12
00:00:49,546 --> 00:00:53,794
It does not give the job an exclusive whole node.

13
00:00:53,794 --> 00:01:00,591
Ordinary jobs should not add --nodes or --exclusive; request whole or multiple nodes only for a

14
00:01:00,591 --> 00:01:03,140
measured application designed to use them.

15
00:01:03,140 --> 00:01:09,736
The first job verifies scheduling, environment capture, output handling and exact comparison.

16
00:01:09,736 --> 00:01:13,742
The second job runs a minimal local Snakemake workflow.

17
00:01:13,742 --> 00:01:17,749
It does not download packages or contact external services.

18
00:01:17,749 --> 00:01:22,386
The third job uses an instructor-provided, immutable training image:

19
00:01:22,386 --> 00:01:27,492
The gate: submits only one job at a time; uses one CPU,

20
00:01:27,492 --> 00:01:34,302
128 MiB RAM and a two-minute limit; refuses job arrays; refuses to run when another learner

21
00:01:34,302 --> 00:01:39,409
gate is active for the same user; waits for a bounded period;

22
00:01:39,409 --> 00:01:46,218
compares output byte-for-byte; cleans only its own temporary directory; does not enumerate nodes or expose scheduler

23
00:01:46,218 --> 00:01:46,643
configuration.

24
00:01:46,643 --> 00:01:52,434
A passing class gate shows that your account can execute the pattern.

25
00:01:52,434 --> 00:01:59,672
It is not a cluster-wide health test and must not be expanded into host-by-host probing.

26
00:01:59,672 --> 00:02:06,428
Reference companion: After completing the bounded gates, use the Slurm command reference for dependencies,

27
00:02:06,428 --> 00:02:10,289
reusable allocations, GPU requests, accounting, cancellation, and checkpointing.

